WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.584
My name is Harold McGee and I'm here today not to tell you how I became a scientist,

00:00:18.585 --> 00:00:21.787
but how I decided not to become a scientist

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:26.425
and still ended up publishing papers in Nature and Physics Today

00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:32.765
except that the subjects were the making of soufflés and the flipping of steaks on a grill.

00:00:32.766 --> 00:00:38.271
So I grew up in the Midwest in the 50s in a brand new subdivision

00:00:38.272 --> 00:00:43.543
where there was still plenty of nature around and the skies were dark.

00:00:43.544 --> 00:00:49.682
And I grew up immersed in that, fascinated by it and was especially fascinated by the stars.

00:00:49.683 --> 00:00:52.151
So I wanted to be an astronomer.

00:00:52.152 --> 00:00:58.124
And my father had gone to Caltech. We had alumni magazines on the coffee table all the time.

00:00:58.125 --> 00:01:02.728
And Caltech ran Mt. Palomar Observatory which at the time was the best.

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:05.331
So, Caltech it was.

00:01:05.332 --> 00:01:10.002
And I went there. Caltech is a science and engineering school.

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:14.307
It's a very small school, a couple hundred students in every graduating class.

00:01:14.308 --> 00:01:18.811
And that meant that the class sizes were small as well.

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:25.852
And that meant that I was able to really get to know a number of wonderful professors,

00:01:25.853 --> 00:01:28.621
including people in the Humanities Departments.

00:01:28.622 --> 00:01:33.793
We had to take philosophy and literature for breadth.

00:01:33.794 --> 00:01:38.731
And what I discovered was, after the first couple of years of Caltech boot camp,

00:01:38.732 --> 00:01:43.803
where everybody takes physics and math and chemistry, and we got into our specialties,

00:01:43.804 --> 00:01:51.511
that I was really getting more interested... or less interested in the nuts and bolts of astronomy,

00:01:51.512 --> 00:01:54.981
the physics of astronomy, and much more interested in

00:01:54.982 --> 00:02:00.653
what astronomy did to our sense of ourselves as creatures on this planet.

00:02:00.654 --> 00:02:07.093
That is to say, I got more interested in the humanities courses than the astronomy courses.

00:02:07.094 --> 00:02:13.766
So, much soul searching, it was difficult to do because nobody at Caltech

00:02:13.767 --> 00:02:15.902
really majored in the humanities,

00:02:15.903 --> 00:02:22.008
I decided to switch gears and to apply for a transfer to a liberal arts school.

00:02:22.009 --> 00:02:27.380
My teachers at Caltech convinced me not to do that.

00:02:27.381 --> 00:02:32.652
And it was the humanities professors who said, "Look, you came to Caltech

00:02:32.653 --> 00:02:34.987
because you love science. You still love science.

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:36.789
You just love it in a different way.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:41.360
And we'll make sure you get a good education in philosophy and literature.

00:02:41.361 --> 00:02:46.299
And if you stay here you can take courses that you'd never be able to take anywhere else

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.668
from the scientists who are here."

00:02:48.669 --> 00:02:54.073
So, that's what I did. And I was able to do things like study

00:02:54.074 --> 00:03:00.713
the synthesis of the elements in stars from the guy who figured that out back in the 50s and 60s.

00:03:00.714 --> 00:03:07.854
And it was an amazing experience. The best decision I made.

00:03:07.855 --> 00:03:13.492
I went from Caltech to Yale to do a Ph.D. in literature.

00:03:13.493 --> 00:03:17.263
I wrote a dissertation about John Keats, a romantic poet.

00:03:17.264 --> 00:03:22.768
And then for a couple of years while I was looking for a tenure track position,

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:30.042
I taught full time at Yale--everything from remedial writing to

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:32.645
the epic tradition from Homer to James Joyce.

00:03:32.646 --> 00:03:37.517
And I loved it. It was great. That was what I wanted to do with my life.

00:03:37.518 --> 00:03:42.121
But, then, as now, tenure track positions were scarce.

00:03:42.122 --> 00:03:45.658
And I didn't have any luck for a couple of years running.

00:03:45.659 --> 00:03:51.831
And so I began to think, "Maybe I should recalibrate here. What can I do?"

00:03:51.832 --> 00:03:58.271
Maybe I can kind of recoup my investment and rediscover my love in science

00:03:58.272 --> 00:04:01.040
and make use of what I'd learned about words

00:04:01.041 --> 00:04:06.179
by teaching literature and teaching writing at Yale and write about science.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:10.249
And maybe write about the science of everyday life,

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.118
which not many people were doing.

00:04:12.119 --> 00:04:20.059
My girlfriend at the time gave an informal class at one of the colleges on the chemistry of fudge making.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:26.699
And it was huge success. People couldn't wait to eat the demonstration afterwards.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:29.402
And so that gave me the idea that maybe

00:04:29.403 --> 00:04:32.672
writing about food and cooking might be an interesting thing to do.

00:04:32.673 --> 00:04:39.178
What sealed the deal was when I mentioned this to a friend of mine, a fellow faculty member,

00:04:39.179 --> 00:04:43.950
who said, "If you're going to write about the science of food and cooking,

00:04:43.951 --> 00:04:48.354
I've got a question for you. I'm from New Orleans. I love red beans and rice.

00:04:48.355 --> 00:04:53.259
I can't eat more than one serving of beans without suffering for it.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:58.497
And so what I want to know is what is it in beans that makes me suffer?

00:04:58.498 --> 00:05:05.238
And is there a hierarchy of beans, some beans that are more painful than others?

00:05:05.239 --> 00:05:10.309
Is there a fart chart that I can refer to to make my life easier?"

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.145
And I thought it was a great question.

00:05:13.146 --> 00:05:16.816
And that sent me off to the library to see if I could find an answer.

00:05:16.817 --> 00:05:23.256
And I went to the food and agriculture section of the science library,

00:05:23.257 --> 00:05:28.528
where I'd never been before and discovered the world of food science,

00:05:28.529 --> 00:05:34.367
which I had no idea existed and which it was clear after a few hours of browsing

00:05:34.368 --> 00:05:44.010
was this amazing trove of information, knowledge, micrographs that blew my mind.

00:05:44.011 --> 00:05:47.580
And I thought, "I have to tell the rest of the world about this stuff!"

00:05:47.581 --> 00:05:52.151
In addition to that, I was able to find the answer to my friend's question,

00:05:52.152 --> 00:05:57.323
but I also discovered that the people who had funded much of the research on flatulence

00:05:57.324 --> 00:06:03.029
was NASA because they were concerned about air quality in space capsules.

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:09.836
So, that taught me that not only was there interesting information, useful information out there,

00:06:09.837 --> 00:06:12.805
there was also a lot of fun to be had with the subject.

00:06:12.806 --> 00:06:18.311
And so, again, some soul searching because I was leaving a job with a paycheck.

00:06:18.312 --> 00:06:25.151
I decided to quit teaching and try to write a book about the science of food and cooking.

00:06:25.152 --> 00:06:28.321
Not food science, but kitchen science.

00:06:28.322 --> 00:06:33.926
And, I did that with no training in either field.

00:06:33.927 --> 00:06:35.895
I didn't know anything about food science.

00:06:35.896 --> 00:06:38.464
I didn't know anything really about culinary arts.

00:06:38.465 --> 00:06:41.801
I cooked at home, but that was it.

00:06:41.802 --> 00:06:45.771
That might sound like a tremendous disadvantage to start from.

00:06:45.772 --> 00:06:49.642
I didn't think about it at all. That's just the way it was.

00:06:49.643 --> 00:06:52.645
And in retrospect, I think it was actually a great thing

00:06:52.646 --> 00:06:55.848
because not knowing anything about either field,

00:06:55.849 --> 00:07:01.254
that meant I had no preconceptions about what either field should be or could do.

00:07:01.255 --> 00:07:06.759
And no preconceptions about what questions were respectable questions,

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:10.997
or interesting questions or questions that could be answered.

00:07:10.998 --> 00:07:16.769
And that I think made it possible for me to write the kind of book that I did.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:23.609
It also led me to experimentation, stuff that I hadn't done since I'd been at Caltech in labs.

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:29.148
So, for example, I read in Julia Child that you should, when you're making an egg white foam,

00:07:29.149 --> 00:07:32.985
to make a meringue or a soufflé, you should use a copper bowl

00:07:32.986 --> 00:07:35.821
because copper bowls give you a better foam.

00:07:35.822 --> 00:07:38.424
And that didn't make any sense to me.

00:07:38.425 --> 00:07:41.994
I went and looked at the food science literature on protein foams.

00:07:41.995 --> 00:07:46.165
There was nothing, nothing in there that was relevant, but I did the experiment.

00:07:46.166 --> 00:07:50.703
I tried, side by side, eggs whipped in a copper and glass bowl,

00:07:50.704 --> 00:07:54.740
and regardless of the chemistry involved, they were tremendously different.

00:07:54.741 --> 00:07:59.478
Just different in texture and color and

00:07:59.479 --> 00:08:04.350
the amount of time it took to get the foam in the first place, and in how long it lasted.

00:08:04.351 --> 00:08:09.522
So, I wanted to know why that was. Food science literature wasn't any help.

00:08:09.523 --> 00:08:11.858
So, I rounded up a couple of scientist friends,

00:08:11.859 --> 00:08:17.029
one of whom had a spectrophotometer that we could use and we came up with a hypothesis.

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:19.532
We tested that hypothesis.

00:08:19.533 --> 00:08:24.570
We wrote a kind of tongue in cheek paper and submitted it to Nature.

00:08:24.571 --> 00:08:28.508
And Nature sent it through its regular review process.

00:08:28.509 --> 00:08:31.944
And it passed with almost flying colors.

00:08:31.945 --> 00:08:38.451
The one qualification that one reviewer made was, "The science looked fine but the subject was fluffy."

00:08:38.452 --> 00:08:41.187
So, Nature went ahead and published it.

00:08:41.188 --> 00:08:48.661
A few years after that I wrote with some physicist friends a paper about

00:08:48.662 --> 00:08:56.235
simulating heat transfer on a computer through foods and discovered, thereby, that

00:08:56.236 --> 00:09:00.606
the faster you flip a steak or a hamburger on the grill,

00:09:00.607 --> 00:09:08.981
the faster it cooks through, significantly faster, and the more evenly it cooks through.

00:09:08.982 --> 00:09:14.754
I would never on my own have thought of flipping a steak every 15 seconds,

00:09:14.755 --> 00:09:18.457
if a computer hadn't told me that it might be a good idea.

00:09:18.458 --> 00:09:21.093
And it turned out that it's a good idea.

00:09:21.094 --> 00:09:26.299
So, I had tremendous fun with the experimental side of this project

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:29.602
as well as just delving into food science proper.

00:09:29.603 --> 00:09:36.075
My book was published in 1984 and it kind of sat there for a few years

00:09:36.076 --> 00:09:38.911
because you have to remember, that was a long time ago,

00:09:38.912 --> 00:09:45.084
that was pre-specialty coffees, pre-extra virgin olive oil, pre-balsamic vinegar,

00:09:45.085 --> 00:09:49.355
pre-personal computer when it comes to doing the scholarship.

00:09:49.356 --> 00:09:55.795
So it took awhile for people to see that the science of cooking was an interesting subject.

00:09:55.796 --> 00:10:00.867
Now, 25 years later, it's actually fashionable.

00:10:00.868 --> 00:10:12.044
Chefs are celebrities and water baths and thermo-couples have made it from the lab into the kitchen.

00:10:12.045 --> 00:10:20.987
And the study of the science of cooking has become of interest not just to cooks, but to scientists.

00:10:20.988 --> 00:10:24.557
So, my book is now being used, the second edition of it,

00:10:24.558 --> 00:10:30.429
it's being used in courses not just at culinary schools, but at universities.

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:35.201
I'm a visiting lecturer at Harvard. I'm a visiting scholar at UC Davis.

00:10:35.202 --> 00:10:38.938
My book's been translated into half a dozen languages.

00:10:38.939 --> 00:10:46.045
And I get to speak to people all over the place about the subject.

00:10:46.046 --> 00:10:50.082
So, it's definitely true that I am not a professional scientist.

00:10:50.083 --> 00:10:54.453
I still don't have tenure, but I have my own library card.

00:10:54.454 --> 00:10:58.090
And I get the chance everyday to share

00:10:58.091 --> 00:11:03.930
the excitement and the fascination of science with lots and lots of other people.

00:11:03.931 --> 00:11:09.702
It's certainly an alternative career, but it's a wonderful alternative,

00:11:09.703 --> 00:11:13.006
and I think there are lots of alternatives like that out there.

